        \begin{itemize}
            \item Before I get into the studies, I want to briefly situate the kind of work I do and how I was trained for it.
            \item At Ateneo de Manila University, I built my foundation in \textbf{psychology}, with strong grounding in \textit{development}, \textit{context}, and \textit{teaching}. My mentor there, Liane Pe\~na-Alampay, is a Penn State HDFS alumna, so that connection makes being here especially meaningful to me.
            \item At Ateneo, I also had the chance to \textbf{teach courses} in statistics, psychometrics, and psychology. That experience shaped how I think about methods: not as ends in themselves, but as tools that should be explainable, useful, and connected to substantive questions.
            \item At the University of Macau, working with Shu Fai Cheung, I deepened my training in \textbf{structural equation modeling}, \textbf{meta-analysis}, and \textbf{statistical computation}.
            \item Then here at Penn State, through PAMT and my work with Sy-Miin Chow and Michael Russell, I extended that foundation into \textbf{dynamic models} and \textbf{intensive longitudinal data}, especially in applications tied to \textit{health} and \textit{behavior} in everyday life.
            \item Together, those experiences shaped the kind of research program I want to build: one that brings developmental science, quantitative methods, and dynamic modeling together to study how change unfolds over time, how it persists, how connected processes influence one another, and how to develop rigorous, reproducible, and genuinely usable tools for substantive researchers.
            \item So before I get into the three studies, I want to start with one simple intuition that ties the whole talk together.
        \end{itemize}
    }
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
	\frametitle{An intuition for inertia}
	\vspace{.5em}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[scale = .20, trim=0 0 0 80, clip]{.setup/latex/figures/png/inertia-running.jpg}
	\begin{center}
		\begin{tikzpicture}
			\node[
				draw=indirect,
				rounded corners=4pt,
				line width=0.8pt,
				fill=white,
				inner xsep=8pt,
				inner ysep=6pt,
				text width=\textwidth,
				align=center
			] {%
				\small
				\textcolor{indirect}{\textbf{Inertia}: how much the past carries forward into the present.}
			};
		\end{tikzpicture}
	\end{center}
	\note{
		\normalsize
		\begin{itemize}
			\item This photo is from the Grand Canyon when I was in graduate school. Around that time, I was also doing a lot of running, and running gave me a simple way to think about dynamics.
			\item In training, your pace today is often shaped by what happened the day before. A hard run can carry forward into the next day through fatigue, while steady training can carry forward through adaptation and momentum.
			\item A lot of the processes we care about in HDFS work the same way: \textbf{they do not reset from one moment to the next}. What happened yesterday, or even a few hours ago, can still shape what happens today.
			\item That is what I mean by \textbf{inertia}: how much the past carries forward into the present.
		\end{itemize}
	}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
	\frametitle{Inertia changes trajectories}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[scale = .80]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/inertia-running-increasing.pdf}
	\vspace{0em}
	\begin{center}
		\begin{tikzpicture}
			\node[
				draw=indirect,
				rounded corners=4pt,
				line width=0.8pt,
				fill=white,
				inner xsep=8pt,
				inner ysep=6pt,
				text width=\textwidth,
				align=center
			] {%
				\small
				\textcolor{indirect}{\textbf{Inertia:} The same starting point can produce very different trajectories when the \textbf{past carries forward} with different strength.}
			};
		\end{tikzpicture}
	\end{center}
	\note{
		\footnotesize
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Here both runners start at the same place, but their trajectories diverge over time.
			\item The difference is not where they began. The difference is how strongly earlier states carry forward.
			\item When inertia is stronger (red curve), the past keeps exerting influence for longer, so the trajectory changes more slowly.
			\item When inertia is weaker (blue curve), the system adjusts more quickly and settles back faster.
			\item That is the key intuition I want to carry into the rest of the talk: dynamic processes are shaped not only by level, but by persistence.
			\item In HDFS, that kind of persistence shows up in affective recovery, stress carryover, sleep, health behavior, and many other daily-life processes.
			\item But dynamic processes are not only self-persistent. They can also shape one another.
		\end{itemize}
	}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
	\frametitle{Coupling and spillover}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[scale = .80]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/dyadic-escalation-spillover.pdf}
	\vspace{0em}
	\begin{center}
		\begin{tikzpicture}
			\node[
				draw=indirect,
				rounded corners=4pt,
				line width=0.8pt,
				fill=white,
				inner xsep=8pt,
				inner ysep=6pt,
				text width=\textwidth,
				align=center
			] {%
				\small
				\textcolor{indirect}{\textbf{Coupling}: change in one process predicts later change in another.}
			};
		\end{tikzpicture}
	\end{center}
	\note{
		\normalsize
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Here, a parent's stress spike is followed by greater child distress. That is the basic idea of \textbf{coupling}: change in one process can predict later change in another.
			\item So now we move from inertia within a single process to spillover across connected processes.
			\item Those two ideas---\textbf{inertia} and \textbf{coupling}---set up the rest of the talk.
		\end{itemize}
	}
\end{frame}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Study 1}
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{frame}
	\frametitle{Study 1}
	\begin{figure}[ht]
		\centering
		% Main figure
		\includegraphics[
			page = 1,
			scale = 0.60,
			trim = 1.50cm 20.75cm 0cm 0cm, % left bottom right top
			clip
		]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/pm-page-1.pdf}
	\end{figure}
	\vspace{0em}
	\begin{center}
		\begin{tikzpicture}
			\node[
				draw=indirect,
				rounded corners=4pt,
				line width=0.8pt,
				fill=white,
				inner xsep=8pt,
				inner ysep=6pt,
				text width=\textwidth,
				align=center
			] {%
				\small
				\textcolor{indirect}{\textbf{Question:} How do mediation effects unfold across time when the process is modeled continuously rather than at one arbitrary lag?}
			};
		\end{tikzpicture}
	\end{center}
	\note{
		\normalsize
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Once we allow processes to persist and to shape one another, a natural next question is whether one process affects another \textbf{through} an intermediate pathway.
			\item So instead of asking only whether stress predicts later mood, we can ask whether part of that effect is transmitted through something like coping.
			\item But as soon as we ask that question over time, the answer can depend on \textbf{when} we look: the same indirect pathway may appear stronger, weaker, or even missed entirely depending on the spacing of the observations.
			\item That is why Study 1 moves to \textbf{continuous time} - to define \textbf{direct}, \textbf{indirect}, and \textbf{total} effects as processes that unfold across time itself, rather than as results tied to one arbitrary observation interval.
		\end{itemize}
	}
\end{frame}

\nocite{Deboeck-Preacher-2015, Ryan-Hamaker-2021, Pesigan-Russell-Chow-2025o}

\begin{frame}
	\frametitle{Why mediation has been a recurring part of my work}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \normalsize \textbf{Substantive} \tiny \parencite[e.g.;][]{Pesigan-Luyckx-Alampay-2014, Bernardo-Wang-Pesigan-etal-2017, Zhang-Ku-Wu-et-al-2020}
		\item \normalsize \textbf{Methodological} \tiny \parencite[e.g.;][]{Pesigan-Sun-Cheung-2023, Pesigan-Cheung-2020, Pesigan-Cheung-2024, Cheung-Pesigan-2023b, Cheung-Pesigan-Vong-2023}.
		      \begin{itemize}
		      	\tiny
		      	\item \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=semmcci}
		      	\item \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=semlbci}
		      	\item \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=betaDelta}
		      	\item \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=betaDSandwich}
		      \end{itemize}
	\end{itemize}
	\vspace{0em}
	\begin{center}
		\begin{tikzpicture}
			\node[
				draw=indirect,
				rounded corners=4pt,
				line width=0.8pt,
				fill=white,
				inner xsep=8pt,
				inner ysep=6pt,
				text width=\textwidth,
				align=center
			] {%
				\small
				\textcolor{indirect}{Study 1 extends that line of work into continuous time by adding \textbf{standardized effect sizes}, principled \textbf{uncertainty quantification}, and an open-source tool in \texttt{cTMed}.}
			};
		\end{tikzpicture}
	\end{center}
	\note{
		\small
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Mediation has been a recurring part of my work from both substantive and methodological angles.
			\item On the substantive side, I have used mediation to study mechanisms in applied problems, including alcohol use and related health behaviors.
			\item On the methods side, I have worked on inference for indirect effects and related quantities, including confidence intervals, bootstrap and Monte Carlo approaches, and standardized effect-size inference.
			\item Study 1 extends that line of work into continuous time.
			\item Prior work showed how these effects unfold across time; my contribution was to make their interpretation and inference more usable.
		\end{itemize}
	}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
	\frametitle{Why this problem matters}
	\begin{minipage}[c]{0.32\textwidth}
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/cross-sectional-mediation.pdf}
		\vspace{0.3em}
		{\scriptsize \textbf{Cross-sectional}\\\tiny One snapshot}
	\end{minipage}\hfill
	\begin{minipage}[c]{0.32\textwidth}
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/longitudinal-mediation.pdf}
		\vspace{0.3em}
		{\scriptsize \textbf{Three-wave}\\\tiny Coarse temporal ordering}
	\end{minipage}\hfill
	\begin{minipage}[c]{0.32\textwidth}
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/dt-var-mediation-bidirectional.pdf}
		\vspace{0.3em}
		{\scriptsize \textbf{Discrete-time vector autoregressive model}\\\tiny Repeated carryover across lags}
	\end{minipage}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \small Mediation is fundamentally a \textbf{temporal process}.
		\item \small But in discrete time, the size of the effect depends on the \textbf{chosen measurement interval}.
	\end{itemize}
	\vspace{0em}
	\begin{center}
		\begin{tikzpicture}
			\node[
				draw=indirect,
				rounded corners=4pt,
				line width=0.8pt,
				fill=white,
				inner xsep=8pt,
				inner ysep=6pt,
				text width=\textwidth,
				align=center
			] {%
				\small
				\textcolor{indirect}{The \textbf{continuous-time} shift is to model the process once, then trace how direct, indirect, and total \textbf{effects unfold over any time interval}, including irregularly spaced designs.}
			};
		\end{tikzpicture}
	\end{center}
	\note{
		\footnotesize
		\begin{itemize}
			\item This slide is really about temporal resolution.
			\item A cross-sectional mediation model is like a single photograph: one snapshot of a process that is actually unfolding over time.
			\item A three-wave model gives us a few still images, so we get some ordering, but only very coarsely.
			\item A discrete-time autoregressive model gets us closer, but it is still like sampling the process at selected frames. What we see depends on which measurement interval we happened to choose.
			\item The continuous-time shift is to model the motion itself rather than just a few snapshots of it.
			\item That lets us trace how the direct, indirect, and total effects unfold over any time interval, including irregularly spaced designs.
			\item For HDFS researchers, that matters because mechanisms in development, family process, and health behavior rarely operate at one arbitrary lag.
		\end{itemize}
	}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
	\frametitle{Novel contributions}
	\begin{minipage}[t]{0.5\textwidth}
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/ct-med.pdf}
		\vspace{0em}
		{\scriptsize \textbf{Effect size measures}\\Standardized total, direct, and indirect effects}
	\end{minipage}\hfill
	\begin{minipage}[t]{0.5\textwidth}
		\centering
		\includegraphics[page = 1,width=\linewidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/ros.pdf}
		\vspace{0em}
		{\scriptsize \textbf{Uncertainty quantification}\\Regions of significance (delta, Monte Carlo, and parametric bootstrap)}
	\end{minipage}\hfill
	\vspace{0em}
	\begin{itemize}
		\tiny
		\item \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=cTMed}
		\item \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=simStateSpace}
		\item \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=bootStateSpace}
	\end{itemize}
	\note{
		\tiny
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Start with the left panel. Think of \textbf{stress} affecting later \textbf{mood}, partly through \textbf{coping}.
			\item The key point is that in continuous time, the direct, indirect, and total effects are not tied to one lag; we can see how the effect of stress on mood unfolds over different intervals.
			\item Early on, the effect of stress on mood is mostly \textbf{direct}. A bit later---around 2 to 4 on the x-axis---more of that effect is carried \textbf{indirectly} through coping.
			\item The \textbf{total} effect of stress on mood peaks a little later and then tapers. These are \textbf{standardized} effects, so they are interpretable much like standardized regression coefficients.
			\item Now look at the right panel. This shows \textbf{uncertainty quantification} for the total effect using the Monte Carlo method.
			\item The shaded region marks the intervals where the total effect of stress on mood is statistically different from zero---roughly around 4 to 8. Outside that range, the interval includes zero.
			\item More broadly, I developed delta, Monte Carlo, and parametric bootstrap approaches for this problem, with Monte Carlo offering a useful middle ground between speed and computational cost.
			\item And these quantities and plots can be generated directly using the open-source \texttt{cTMed} package.
		\end{itemize}
	}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
	\frametitle{One direction this line of work opens up}
	\begin{minipage}[t]{0.5\textwidth}
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/total-effect-centrality.pdf}
												
		\vspace{0.3em}
												
		{\scriptsize \textbf{Total effect centrality}\\ \tiny Which variable matters most for shaping the rest of the system over time?}
	\end{minipage}\hfill
	\begin{minipage}[t]{0.5\textwidth}
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{.setup/latex/figures/pdf/indirect-effect-centrality.pdf}
												
		\vspace{0.3em}
												
		{\scriptsize \textbf{Indirect effect centrality}\\ \tiny Which variable matters most as the pathway through which effects spread over time?}
	\end{minipage}\hfill
	\vspace{0em}
	\begin{center}
		\begin{tikzpicture}
			\node[
				draw=indirect,
				rounded corners=4pt,
				line width=0.8pt,
				fill=white,
				inner xsep=8pt,
				inner ysep=6pt,
				text width=\textwidth,
				align=center
			] {%
				\normalsize
				\textcolor{indirect}{This framework can also be extended from single mediated pathways to \textbf{system-level summaries} of how effects spread through dynamic networks over time.}
			};
		\end{tikzpicture}
	\end{center}
	\tiny Ongoing work on person-specific affect networks: Pesigan, Russell, Chow, Fisher, \& Barbara L. Fredrickson, invited submission to an \textit{Emotion} special issue.
	\note{
		\tiny
		\begin{itemize}
			\item One reason I am excited about this work is that it opens up a broader systems perspective.
			\item Study 1 focused on mediation one pathway at a time, but many substantive questions are really about how an \textbf{entire system reorganizes} over time.
			\item That is the idea behind an ongoing project on \textbf{person-specific affect networks during meditation training}, across baseline, intervention, and follow-up.
			\item Here, \textbf{total-effect centrality} asks which emotion has the \textit{broadest downstream} influence, and \textbf{indirect-effect centrality} asks which emotion serves as a key \textit{conduit} through which effects spread.
			\item Because the model is continuous-time, we can examine those roles across \textbf{multiple timescales}---from short-term ripple effects to longer-horizon accumulation.
			\item And because this is an intervention study, we can link that reorganization to \textbf{meditation practice dose} and to \textbf{follow-up affective functioning and well-being}.
			\item So the broader payoff of Study 1 is a framework for turning complex dynamics into interpretable summaries that show \textbf{which variables have the widest downstream influence and which variables serve as the main pathways through which effects spread over time}.
			\item And once we can describe dynamic systems that way, the next question is whether those dynamics vary meaningfully across individuals.
		\end{itemize}
	}
\end{frame}

